# Code guideline modules (index)

These **`.mdc`** files govern **architectural patterns** for Rust device
daemons. Consumed via git submodule at **`.cursor/rules/`**.

**Product-specific** requirements, ADRs, SoC SKUs, schema catalogs, and
topology locks belong in each consumer repo under **`docs/specification/`**.

## Process rules

| File | Topic |
|------|--------|
| **`spec-driven-development.mdc`** | SDD truth hierarchy, as-built discipline, PR traceability |
| **`testing-verify-flows.mdc`** | cargo test / verify / device-tier methodology |
| **`rust-device-adr.mdc`** | ADR triggers, template |

## Architecture & runtime

| File | Topic |
|------|--------|
| **`architecture.mdc`** | Core crate vs platform backends; no product `#ifdef` trees |
| **`platform-backends.mdc`** | Trait boundaries; mac / linux / jetson implement |
| **`process-lifecycle.mdc`** | Long-lived service unit; construct vs run |
| **`local-ipc.mdc`** | Local IPC transport craft; bounded queues |
| **`resource-governance.mdc`** | Footprint caps, thresholded telemetry, backpressure |
| **`cloud-adapter.mdc`** | Cloud transport adapter — no domain logic |
| **`credential-store.mdc`** | How to store secrets; no secret logging |
| **`dependency-injection.mdc`** | One DI style; container-owned singleton concept |
| **`fail-fast.mdc`** | Fail fast; no silent recovery without approval |
| **`logging-observability.mdc`** | Structured logs; correlation; no secrets |
| **`rust-tooling.mdc`** | cargo fmt / clippy / test; edition policy |

## Per-repository product documentation

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **`docs/specification/product/`** | Requirements, capabilities |
| **`docs/specification/adr/`** | Architecture decisions (topology, schemas, backends) |
| **`docs/specification/as-built/`** | What is live today |
| **`tests/README.md`** | Verify commands |

## Cross-project conventions

- **Do not** add product features or tenant names to shared **`.mdc`** files.
- **Do not** edit **`.cursor/rules/`** in consumer repos — PR upstream here.
- Practice **filenames** follow `python-services-rules` where the practice is shared.
